  This breaks table rendering and is clearly a copy-paste/format defect (compare to the properly-piped Codex/Gemini rows on 198-200). Why it matters: the Artifact Map is a navigation surface; a malformed row there is exactly the kind of noise this plan is trying to eliminate from tier-1 routing docs.

- [MINOR] **Self-cited plan mtime is stale inside the plan** — lines 76 and 79 state `current plan mtime 2026-04-23 11:12:39 -0500`, but `stat` on the live file reports `2026-04-23 12:50:09 -0500`. The plan was edited again after the evidence block was written. The directional claim (marker older than plan) is still correct, so this is not approval-blocking, but the self-citation is internally falsified and will trip any reviewer who spot-checks. Why it matters: the plan uses its own mtime as evidence of the staleness gap; stale self-evidence weakens that claim.

- [MINOR] **r16 review on an r11-last-reviewed lineage, with no r12-r15 bridge** — the plan's Adversarial Review Summary (lines 371-377) and status header still reference `r11` as the latest completed rerun, but the current prompt (`.planning/quick/review-2460-r16-prompt.md`) treats this as the r16 rerun. Either r12-r15 were skipped/silent (so the plan should say so) or r12-r15 artifacts exist but aren't cited. Currently `ls scripts/review/results/ | grep 2460` returns only r11-era files. Why it matters: Codex r11 already flagged "review-state narrative is still internally inconsistent"; a 5-rev gap with no bridging narrative re-opens that failure mode.

- [MINOR] **Governance Precondition title still reads as conditional** — line 356 heading `## Governance Precondition Before Any Future Approval Reuse` frames cleanup as guarding *future* reuse, even though line 360's body ("must be removed, superseded, or revision-bound before this draft can be treated as approved again") is correctly present-tense. This was exactly the Codex r11 MAJOR finding ("cleanup requirement is still conditional on a future MAJOR rerun rather than a present requirement"). Retitling to something like `## Stale Approval Surfaces — Present Cleanup Requirement` would close the loop. Why it matters: harness orchestrators and humans skim section headings; a "Future" framing in the title undercuts the present-tense body.

- [MINOR] **Status-header self-reference is ambiguous across revisions** — line 3 says "*latest completed 2026-04-23 reruns still show this draft lineage is not approval-ready… the current draft remains pending a clean external rerun*." "This draft lineage" vs "the current draft" are treated as distinct entities, but the plan never defines what separates them (file revision? git SHA?). Without a revision identifier, a reviewer cannot verify whether the completed r11 reruns apply to the on-disk bytes being reviewed now. The Governance Precondition's revision-bind clause (line 361) correctly requires an "exact approved plan git commit SHA or SHA256 hash" — the same discipline should be applied retroactively to the status header so "lineage" has a deterministic identity.

- [MINOR] **Plan references `#2465` as follow-through without explicit linkage to the freshness-report refresh workflow** — the Acceptance Criteria (line 345) requires the contract to "require refreshing or regenerating `docs/reports/tier-1-indexing-freshness-latest.md`" and "explicitly name `#2465` as the follow-through issue," but there is no test or acceptance item that guards against an implementer merely mentioning `#2465` in an unrelated paragraph. The TDD entry `test_tier1_contract_marks_2465_as_daily_freshness_followthrough` (line 327) verifies linkage presence, not co-location in the freshness section. The pseudocode (line 239) does say "name #2465 in the same freshness section as the follow-through issue" — consider hardening the test name/description to match the pseudocode's co-location requirement.

